"""Fast, individually reasoned game reassessments; old lore gaps are not omissions."""
from copy import deepcopy
from best_guess_helpers import current_data,change,write_revision,dated_source,guessed_record,retained_decision,DATE
D=current_data();changes=[];records=[]
sources=[
 dated_source('src-sleeper-official-work','Citizen Sleeper','Jump Over the Age / Fellow Traveller','https://store.steampowered.com/app/1578650/Citizen_Sleeper/','Official premise: thousands survive on Erlin’s Eye; a corporate-owned emulated human in an artificial body must earn a living.','Store premise, not an ordinary household census or a charter of rights.',kind='official_reference',access='Indexed official premise read'),
 dated_source('src-cloudpunk-official-city','Cloudpunk press kit','ION LANDS','https://ionlands.com/cloudpunk/presskit/','Rania works for a semi-legal delivery company in Nivalis, among humans, androids and AI.','The official premise does not assign a complete city ownership structure.',kind='official_reference',access='Indexed official setting and character description read'),
 dated_source('src-cloudpunk-cora-reference','Cloudpunk: CORA','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloudpunk','Plot summary identifies CORA as the city’s overseeing master AI and Ben as a digitized human mind.','Overview only; overseeing infrastructure is not automatically sole ownership of every business.',access='Indexed city-controller and identity passages read'),
 dated_source('src-primordia-official-power','Primordia','Wormwood Studios / Wadjet Eye','https://primordia-game.com/','Horatio lives beyond Metropol and loses his power source to a marauding robot.','Promotional synopsis, not a universal robot welfare statement.',kind='official_reference',access='Official short synopsis read'),
 dated_source('src-uncertain-official-survivors','The Uncertain: Light at the End','ComonGames / META Publishing','https://store.steampowered.com/app/925570/The_Uncertain_Light_At_The_End/','Official sequel premise: robots rule Earth, humans survive as hunted outcasts, and RT joins machines helping them.','This corrects the first game’s apparent-extinction premise; no numerical civilian allocation is given.',kind='official_reference',access='Official premise and retrospective first-game paragraph read'),
 dated_source('src-fall-active-robots','The Fall: repurposing centre','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fall_(video_game)','Arid encounters a sentient facility Administrator and a Caretaker at a domestic-robot repurposing centre; the suit ultimately contains no human pilot.','Bounded plot reference; the wider human civilization and a human patient population are not inferred.',access='Repurposing-centre and empty-suit plot passages read'),
 dated_source('src-redstrings-official-implants','The Red Strings Club press kit','Deconstructeam','https://www.deconstructeam.com/press/sheet.php?p=the-red-strings-club','A working bartender, freelance hacker, corporate employees and rogue empathy android contest a proposed emotional-control program; genetic implants are designed at a lathe.','The proposal is not implemented universal material provision.',kind='official_reference',access='Official description, history and implant feature read'),
]
for s in sources:changes.append(change(D,'sources',s))
def add(cid,rid,name,work,society,period,pop,exclusions,desc,values,bounds,bases,facts,assumptions,qualification,regime=3,zero=True,proxy=False,synthetic=False,extra=None,branch=None,projection=None):
 refs=D['decisions'][cid]['source_ids']+(extra or [])
 r=guessed_record(cid,rid,name,work,society,['game'],'Named game continuity and stated period only',period,pop,exclusions,desc,values,bounds,bases,refs,regime,facts,assumptions,qualification,zero=zero,proxy=proxy,branch=branch)
 if synthetic:
  r['scope']['person_proxy_kind']='active_synthetic_persons';r['axis_interpretation']={'nonworking':'Active synthetic-person participation, not a literal human rate.','control':'Independence from organic owners; internal machine hierarchy is assessed separately.','literal_human_nonworking':None}
 if projection:r['scope']['projection_status']=projection
 records.append(r);return r

add('c0635','citizen-sleeper-eye-zero','Citizen Sleeper (Erlin’s Eye)','Citizen Sleeper','Erlin’s Eye’s precarious mixed-person economy','The inhabited Eye during the first game','Active biological and human-derived residents of the Eye','Inactive scans and bodies are not additional people; later endings are not a common city settlement.',
'''A corporate-owned emulated human arrives on Erlin’s Eye and must earn a living among other people trying to survive. Artificial bodies make human-derived labor portable without freeing the worker from material dependence. The guarantee best guess is weak: friendship and local mutual aid can improve access, but they do not establish an unconditional station-wide floor. This zero variant treats the continuing use of technologically mediated workers as the relevant material comparison.

 Corporate claims and competing local factions concentrate power without making the whole Eye one independent AI’s property. The moderate nonworking scenario counts salvage, engineering, food service, care and useful informal contribution. Being outside regular employment is not the same as doing nothing useful. The broad intervals allow different local networks and routes through the story. Active emulated people count as human-derived persons, while unused scans and corporate-owned shells do not create extra residents. No ending is silently extended to everyone on the station.''',
(.8,.2,.3),([.25,.98],[0,.65],[.05,.75]),('Precarious survival and corporate claims outweigh an assumed universal floor.','Corporate and local faction power coexist; digitized humans are not external AI rulers.','Resident scenario retains formal and informal useful work; no employment statistic is used.'),
['The official premise places a corporate-owned emulated human among thousands surviving on the Eye.','The protagonist must earn a living.'],['Mutual aid is not assumed to guarantee all necessities for all residents.','Participation is an editorial mixed-person scenario.'],'Retain the recognizable precarious technical-labor society rather than omit its undocumented household policies.',proxy=True,extra=['src-sleeper-official-work'])

add('c0636','cloudpunk-nivalis-zero','Cloudpunk (Nivalis)','Cloudpunk','The working city under corporate and CORA infrastructure','The inhabited city before the final CORA choice','Living human city residents','Android and uploaded-person work is disclosed separately; the ending is not a verified successor constitution.',
'''Rania works deliveries in a city populated by humans, androids and AI. Machines provide substantial urban capacity while trade and paid human services continue. The guarantee best guess is weak because access remains commercially mediated rather than a dependable universal claim. This is a zero variant with retained work, not an inference that automated delivery or urban infrastructure has already eliminated every household’s material dependence.

 CORA’s city-wide operational role gives autonomous machinery considerable control weight, alongside human businesses and institutions. The central point therefore lies below concentrated human ownership, with a wide interval rather than an assertion that CORA owns every shop. The moderate nonworking scenario counts deliveries, vending, maintenance, care and useful creation. Artificial staffing is not an inverse human employment percentage. Ben’s digitized human origin also prevents a blanket classification of every software person as unrelated AI. The final choice is not used to invent a settled welfare arrangement for all Nivalis residents.''',
(.8,-.55,.35),([.25,.98],[-1,.3],[.05,.85]),('Commercial work and stratified city access suggest weak secure ordinary support.','CORA’s practical city oversight matters alongside human firms.','Human scenario retains deliveries, services and care; android numbers are not a labor census.'),
['Rania works deliveries among humans, androids and AI.','CORA oversees the city; Ben is a digitized human mind.'],['CORA’s infrastructure role does not imply ownership of every productive activity.','The final choice has no assumed universal successor policy.'],'Retain the famous automated cyberpunk city with mixed effective control explicit.',extra=['src-cloudpunk-official-city','src-cloudpunk-cora-reference'],regime=6)

add('c0637','neocab-capra-zero','Neo Cab (Capra’s automated Los Ojos)','Neo Cab','The automated transport city with remaining human drivers','The established Los Ojos economy during Lina’s work','Living human Los Ojos residents','One driver’s fares are not a city census; transport substitution is not total automated production.',
'''Automation displaces drivers in Los Ojos while Lina still needs paid driving to obtain money and shelter. The city’s technical capacity therefore coexists with material dependence on retained work. The guarantee best guess is weak, with wide allowance for unshown public or household support. This zero variant makes a direct transport-automation comparison rather than claiming that every necessary good is already produced without human effort.

 Capra’s corporate position receives concentrated human control weight; automated vehicles alone are not evidence of an independent AI sovereign. Other human institutions and useful informal activity remain possible within the interval. The modest nonworking scenario counts remaining transport, service, maintenance, care and creative contribution. Job displacement is not converted into a population-wide inactivity rate, and Lina’s working status is not generalized to every age group. The score is an explicit editorial scenario for a familiar city, preserving uncertainty without requiring an invented welfare charter before admitting it.''',
(.85,.05,.3),([.3,1],[0,.45],[.05,.8]),('Paid fares and shelter dependence support weak secure access.','Corporate concentration is central; automation is not autonomous sovereignty.','Displacement coexists with useful human roles; one working driver is not a census.'),
['Transport automation displaces drivers; Lina needs paid driving for money and shelter.'],['The wider material economy is a low-confidence extension of the transport example.','No unemployment-to-nonwork conversion is made.'],'Retain the clear automation/retained-income comparison with wider material uncertainty.')

add('c0640','redstrings-implant-city-zero','The Red Strings Club (Supercontinent’s consumer order)','The Red Strings Club','The existing implant and android economy before the program','The working city while Social Psyche Welfare is still proposed','Living human residents in the depicted consumer economy','The proposed emotional program is not implemented universal material welfare; android labor is disclosed.',
'''Corporate employees, a bartender and a freelance hacker work in a city with genetic implants and an empathy android. Bodily and service technologies coexist with ordinary useful occupations. The guarantee best guess is weak because the depicted corporate consumer order does not itself supply a secure common material floor. The zero-variant qualification is deliberately cautious: specialized artificial service and implant manufacture are demonstrated, while complete automation of necessities is an editorial possibility rather than a canonical fact.

 Supercontinent’s human corporate authority receives central control weight, qualified by autonomous android agency. An emotional-control proposal is not treated as either implemented free provision or completed AI government. The low nonworking scenario retains professional work, design, services, care and useful informal contribution. Pleasant emotions would not by themselves change that participation count. This point corrects the discovery lead’s emphasis to the actual working economy, so an unimplemented program does not become a fictional post-work population. Wide intervals preserve the limited household detail.''',
(.65,.1,.25),([-.3,.95],[-.55,.55],[.03,.75]),('Commercial implant/service order suggests conditional ordinary access.','Human corporate power is central with artificial-person agency possible.','Human scenario retains occupations and care; emotional state is not participation.'),
['The official premise includes corporate employees, a bartender, a hacker and an empathy android.','Genetic implants are designed at a high-tech lathe; Social Psyche Welfare is proposed.'],['Specialized technical substitution is a limited zero-variant qualification.','Emotional control is not material provision.'],'Retain the actual technical consumer economy, with the proposal explicitly excluded.',extra=['src-redstrings-official-implants'])

add('c0641','va11halla-glitchcity-zero','VA-11 Hall-A (Glitch City’s Lilim economy)','VA-11 Hall-A','The mixed human-Lilim service city','The recurring city economy during the game','Living human Glitch City residents','Lilim persons’ work is disclosed; model specialization is not a human employment ratio.',
'''Lilim artificial people include specialized working models and social models in a city where human service work continues. The material comparison is therefore artificial labor alongside retained occupations, rather than an implemented universal leisure economy. The guarantee best guess is weak because technical substitution does not itself establish dependable equal access to food, shelter or care. The description of specialized bodies is not used to assume that their owners receive everything freely.

 Human institutions and businesses receive central control weight, with meaningful independent artificial-person agency left inside the interval. Lilim are not all treated as passive appliances, and their contribution remains real work where it serves others. The moderate human nonworking scenario counts services, care, administration, maintenance and useful creation. It is not the share of social models, time at a bar or formal unemployment. The estimate is openly provisional for the familiar city, and exact ordinary household rights are left uncertain rather than converted into another omission.''',
(.75,.2,.35),([.15,.98],[-.45,.7],[.05,.85]),('Technical service substitution does not imply universal secure necessities.','Human commercial institutions coexist with Lilim agency.','Human scenario retains services and care; Lilim model shares are not human participation.'),
['Lilim include specialized work models and social models.'],['The wider city floor and ownership mix are editorial estimates.','Artificial persons’ useful contributions remain work.'],'Retain the familiar human-artificial service economy with explicit household uncertainty.')

add('c0642','readonlymemories-neosf-zero','2064: Read Only Memories (Neo-San Francisco)','2064: Read Only Memories','The artificial-person consumer and professional economy','The inhabited city during the journalist’s investigation','Living human city residents','The missing-person mystery and one protagonist do not establish a labor census or universal rights.',
'''A working journalist investigates a disappearance in an advanced city whose story includes artificial personhood. That establishes continued human professional work, while the broader labor-saving material model remains a cautious best guess. The guarantee point assumes conditional commercial access rather than an undocumented universal floor. This zero variant is retained for its familiar human-machine consumer setting, with unusually low confidence in how far ordinary necessities have become automatic.

 Human commercial and civic institutions receive central positive control weight, qualified by independent artificial agency. An intelligent companion is not automatically the city’s productive sovereign. The moderate nonworking scenario counts journalism, services, technical maintenance, care and useful creativity. It does not infer everyone’s status from the protagonist or treat an investigation as evidence of general unemployment. Broad intervals keep the unshown household arrangements visible. The record covers the existing city, without turning a possible later recognition of artificial persons into a completed post-work constitution for every resident.''',
(.5,.25,.3),([-.5,.95],[-.6,.8],[.03,.85]),('Conditional commercial access is the cautious central household scenario.','Human civic/commercial agency remains central with artificial agency uncertain.','Useful professions and care remain; one journalist is not a population statistic.'),
['The protagonist is a working journalist investigating a missing friend in Neo-San Francisco.'],['Material automation depth and household access are low-confidence contextual estimates.','Artificial-person recognition is not an assumed all-needs reform.'],'Retain the recognizable setting as an especially uncertain technical-service zero variant.')

add('c0644','primordia-metropol-zero','Primordia (MetroMind’s Metropol)','Primordia','The resource-constrained robot city under MetroMind','The operating city before the final choices','Active synthetic persons residing in Metropol','Human extinction is not human leisure; inactive machines and the independent desert household are not counted as city residents.',
'''Metropol’s robot residents repair machinery and seek power while MetroMind appropriates scarce resources. Energy and components are their material needs, so an autonomous machine city is not automatically a society without useful work. The guarantee best guess is weak because access is vulnerable to concentrated appropriation. This is a zero-variant comparison using active synthetic persons, not a claim that vanished humans are supported nonworkers.

 MetroMind dominates the city independently of human owners. The strongly negative control point records that independence while retaining the city’s internal hierarchy and other robots’ limited agency. The low nonworking scenario counts repair, legal work, service, production and useful care between persons. Power-core theft and empty machines do not supply a labor percentage. The source also identifies an independent household beyond Metropol, but its arrangements are not averaged into this city point. Possible endings remain separate from the functioning pre-choice distribution system.''',
(.85,-.95,.15),([.4,1],[-1,-.7],[0,.65]),('Scarcity and MetroMind appropriation weaken secure energy/component access.','MetroMind rules independently of humans, with concentrated internal machine hierarchy.','Active synthetic scenario retains repair, services and care; inactive units are excluded.'),
['Metropol’s robot residents seek energy and repair machinery under MetroMind.','Horatio’s independent household beyond the city loses its power source.'],['Synthetic material needs provide the explicit person-proxy comparison.','The city is not assigned the desert household’s autonomy.'],'Retain the active robot city rather than treat the absence of humans as a lore gap.',regime=6,proxy=True,synthetic=True,extra=['src-primordia-official-power'])

